{
  "name": "Picket",
  "notes": "One `line` that never moves, drawn as a moving fence. `tile` turns a single shape into N copies, `translate` slides the tiled coordinate so the whole set travels, and `mirror` folds it so the fence approaching from one side meets itself coming back. Three warps, all modulated, on an emitter whose `centre` is 0.5 for the entire patch -- every bit of motion you see is the space moving under a stationary object, which is the cheapest complex animation the engine can produce: one trapezoid integration per pixel however many pickets the tile makes.\n\nThe count is on a triangle from three to eleven and it is a float, so between integers the last cell is cut short and a discontinuity travels through the fence once per integer crossed. That is the same artefact `18-phaser` uses deliberately; here it reads as a post being added or removed while you watch.\n\nThe chain is `M3 -> M1.rate`, so the scroll surges and stalls rather than running at a constant speed, and the fence occasionally hangs almost still while the count is still changing under it. `index: \"position\"` means the palette is fixed to the STRIP rather than to the pickets, so a post changes colour as it travels rather than carrying one -- the fence is a moving window onto a stationary gradient. Ember run with a single cobalt accent that only the far end of the strip holds.",
  "layers": {
    "0": {
      "emitter": {
        "type": "line",
        "params": {
          "centre": 0.5,
          "length": 0.055,
          "softness": 0
        }
      },
      "colour": {
        "index": "position",
        "wrap": true,
        "palette": {
          "params": {
            "saturation": 2,
            "value": 2
          },
          "entries": [
            "ff0028",
            "ff5000",
            "ffaa00",
            "0064ff"
          ]
        }
      },
